 <string>&lt;p&gt; a) PrimeMover(a) &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt;b) ∃x(PrimeMover(x)&amp;amp;∀y(PrimeMover(y)→y=x)) &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt;There is a Prime Mover, x, and for whatever y you choose, if y is a Prime Mover then y=x.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;c) Now change the diagram. Click on the selection arrow, top left. Select the PrimeMover rectangle and move it slightly so as to move either a or b outside the rectangle. Have a look the Interpretation. Now run question (b) a second time i.e.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt; d) ∃x(PrimeMover(x)&amp;amp;∀y(PrimeMover(y)→y=x)) &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt;N.B. This shows the difference between &quot;a&quot; and &quot;the&quot;.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt; e) ∃x∃y(PrimeMover(x)&amp;amp;PrimeMover(y)&amp;amp;~(x=y)&amp;amp;∀w(PrimeMover(w)→(w=x)∨(w=y))) &lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt;Try Endorse-Denying this formula using the Interpretion you now have. Then press the button Reset Drawing and Try Endorse-Denying this formula a second time. &lt;/p&gt;</string>
